# Dates from engine/notation/chronicle.ts: Baghdad 762, the House of Wisdom under al-Ma'mun 813 to
# 833, Heian-kyo 794, the Song from 960, Genji by 1008, St Sophia in Kyiv 1011 or 1037, the walls
# of Benin from about 800, the Norse at L'Anse aux Meadows 1021.
lesson history-the-world-in-the-year-1000 v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=1 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "The world in the year 1000"
  goal "Find five great cities of the year 1000 on the map, say what each is known for, and count how old each was."

  look {
    scene 36x17 {
      worldmap map names=0 blank=-1 oceans=0 places=["Baghdad", "Kaifeng", "Kyoto", "Kyiv", "Benin City"] numbered=0 route=0 at=canvas(1, 0)
    }
    say """
      Around the year 1000 people lived very differently in different places. In Baghdad, founded in
      762, scholars in the House of Wisdom copied and translated books. In Kaifeng, the Song capital,
      markets stayed open late into the night. In Kyoto, Murasaki Shikibu wrote The Tale of Genji.
      In Kyiv, a great church was built. Benin City was ringed by long walls of earth. And far to the
      west, Norse sailors reached America.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.y1000-continent k=0
    }
    show history.y1000-continent k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show history.y1000-which-city k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.y1000-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.y1000-how-old k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.y1000-continent k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show history.y1000-which-city k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.y1000-order k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.y1000-how-old k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    level hard {
      show history.y1000-which-city k=3 level=medium
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.y1000-why-books k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.y1000-youngest k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "In the year 1000 there were great cities on several continents at once: Baghdad, Kaifeng, Kyoto, Kyiv and Benin City."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is to see one moment across the whole world rather than one country's story: in the
    year 1000 there were great cities in Asia, Europe and Africa, each with its own learning, trade
    and buildings. The way in finds a city's continent; the core matches cities to what they are
    known for, orders their founding, and counts how old each was. The wrong idea to watch for is
    that history happened in one place at a time. If the child is stuck, put a finger on each city
    and say one thing about it. Off the page, find the year 1000 in a book or museum of your own
    country, and what was happening there. England's key stage 2 history asks for "a non-European
    society that provides contrasts with British history", naming "early Islamic civilization,
    including a study of Baghdad c. AD 900" and "Benin (West Africa) c. AD 900-1300". Russia's
    history programme for grade six includes «Арабский халифат... Культура исламского мира.
    Образование и наука» and «Япония в Средние века».
    Japan's 社会 takes up history only through Japan's own story, in grade six
    (「我が国の歴史上の主な事象について，人物の働きや代表的な文化遺産を中心に」, the course of study as read in its 2008 edition), so
    this lesson has no line of its own there; its timeline and source skills serve that grade.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example finds Kyiv's continent, the cities are matched to their plainest facts, the founding dates are far apart, and the years counted run to 1021."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the continents' names are left off the map, the cities are matched to al-Khwarizmi and Benin's walls, the founding dates are muddled, and the years counted are between the capitals themselves."
  }
}
